  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1607.05561v1</id>
    <title>On the abelianity of the stochastic sandpile model</title>
    <updated>2016-07-19T13:03:28Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.05561v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05561v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We consider a stochastic variant of the Abelian Sandpile Model (ASM) on a finite graph, introduced by Chan, Marckert and Selig. Even though it is a more general model, some nice properties still hold. We show that on a certain probability space, even if we lose the group structure due to topplings not being deterministic, some operators still commute. As a corollary, we show that the stationary distribution still does not depend on how sand grains are added onto the graph in our model, answering a conjecture of Selig.</summary>
    <category term="math.CO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2016-07-19T13:03:28Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>14 pages, 3 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="math.CO"/>
    <author>
      <name>François Nunzi</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9904075v2</id>
    <title>On the scaling behavior of the abelian sandpile model</title>
    <updated>1999-05-18T15:19:13Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9904075v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9904075v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>  The abelian sandpile model in two dimensions does not show the type of critical behavior familar from equilibrium systems. Rather, the properties of the stationary state follow from the condition that an avalanche started at a distance r from the system boundary has a probability proportional to 1/sqrt(r) to reach the boundary. As a consequence, the scaling behavior of the model can be obtained from evaluating dissipative avalanches alone, allowing not only to determine the values of all exponents, but showing also the breakdown of finite-size scaling.</summary>
    <category term="cond-mat.dis-nn" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cond-mat.soft" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cond-mat.stat-mech" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>1999-04-06T16:50:10Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>4 pages, 5 figures; the new version takes into account that the radius distribution of avalanches cannot become steeper than a certain power law</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cond-mat.dis-nn"/>
    <author>
      <name>Barbara Drossel</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.1103/PhysRevE.61.R2168</arxiv:doi>
    <link rel="related" href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.61.R2168" title="doi"/>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07258v2</id>
    <title>The divisible sandpile at critical density</title>
    <updated>2015-08-13T01:15:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07258v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.07258v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The divisible sandpile starts with i.i.d. random variables ("masses") at the vertices of an infinite, vertex-transitive graph, and redistributes mass by a local toppling rule in an attempt to make all masses at most 1. The process stabilizes almost surely if m&lt;1 and it almost surely does not stabilize if m&gt;1, where $m$ is the mean mass per vertex. The main result of this paper is that in the critical case m=1, if the initial masses have finite variance, then the process almost surely does not stabilize. To give quantitative estimates on a finite graph, we relate the number of topplings to a discrete biLaplacian Gaussian field.</summary>
    <category term="math.PR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cond-mat.stat-mech" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="math.AP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2015-01-28T20:15:39Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>34 pages, to appear in Annales Henri Poincare</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="math.PR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Lionel Levine</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mathav Murugan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuval Peres</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Baris Evren Ugurcan</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.1007/s00023-015-0433-x</arxiv:doi>
